DevOps Scenario Series

Scenario (K8s):
A fresher joined a team. He deployed a pod, but after restart the pod got a new name and new IP. He got confused:
    “Why pod name/IP changes after restart? How do I give my app a fixed access point?”


Solution:
Pods are ephemeral (short-lived). Restart = new pod = new IP.
For a stable endpoint, we use a Service.

Example Service:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: myapp-svc
spec:
  selector:
    app: myapp
  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8080
  type: ClusterIP

Now no matter how many times pods restart, app is always reachable at myapp-svc.


Interview Quickies
       Q - Why pod IP is not reliable?
       A - Because pods are temporary, they get new IPs when recreated.
       Q - How do you give a fixed identity to pods?
       A - By attaching a Service, which gives a stable DNS name & virtual IP.     
       Q - Types of Service?
       A - ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer.
